      "id": "minamata-mercury",
      "title": "Minamata connected factory to fish",
      "question": "Why did proof lag?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Minamata disease surfaced during 1956. Fishing families developed neurological illness. Kumamoto researchers implicated seafood quickly. Cats reproduced similar symptoms. Organic mercury emerged during 1959. Chisso produced acetaldehyde nearby. Its process created methylmercury. Effluent entered Minamata Bay. Fish concentrated that pollutant. Residents then ate those fish. Chisso continued discharging wastewater. Production stopped during 1968. Government recognition followed that year. **Benefits claimed.** Acetaldehyde supported industrial growth. Chisso sustained local employment. Better testing required time. **Scholarly interpretation.** Uncertainty became institutional delay. Economic dependence narrowed official action. Company opposition slowed acceptance. Food-chain evidence changed toxicology. **Counterevidence and limits.** Early instruments had limits. Several toxins appeared possible. Researchers still identified seafood early. Fishing restrictions could reduce exposure. Exact victim totals remain contested. Certification rules exclude some claimants. Later recognition cannot erase uncertainty. It does establish causation. The pathway remains documented. Public trust collapsed.",
