      "id": "thalidomide-regulatory-barrier",
      "title": "Thalidomide met one regulatory barrier",
      "question": "What prevented wider harm?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Thalidomide spread during the 1950s. Many countries approved its sale. Pregnant patients received it. Severe birth defects followed. Thousands of children were affected. Merrell sought American approval. FDA reviewer Frances Kelsey objected. Safety evidence remained inadequate. Neuropathy reports increased concern. Kelsey requested stronger data. Approval never arrived. Yet investigational distribution still occurred. Some American pregnancies were exposed. Congress strengthened drug regulation afterward. **Benefits claimed.** Manufacturers promoted effective sedation. Nausea treatment promised relief. Later uses proved medically valuable. Thalidomide treats multiple myeloma. It also treats leprosy complications. **Scholarly interpretation.** Regulatory doubt prevented wider exposure. International tragedy changed evidence rules. Kelsey became its symbol. **Counterevidence and limits.** Kelsey never acted alone. FDA colleagues supported continued review. Existing law enabled repeated delays. European clinicians detected fetal harm. American exposure was not zero. Animal testing then remained limited. Modern surveillance still matters. The drug itself has benefits. Risk depends upon pregnancy. Strong controls remain essential.",
      "summary": "Skepticism blocked wider exposure.",
