      "id": "great-smog-1952",
      "title": "Weather trapped London’s coal smoke",
      "question": "Was weather the cause?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** London turned cold during 1952. Homes burned more coal. Factories also emitted pollution. An anticyclone created inversion. Warm air capped colder air. Smoke could not disperse. Fog droplets captured particles. Sulphur chemistry worsened toxicity. Visibility collapsed across London. Transport also stopped. Death registrations rose sharply. Officials counted roughly four thousand. Later estimates were higher. Exact totals remain uncertain. Parliament passed cleaner-air laws. The first arrived during 1956. Another followed during 1968. **Benefits claimed.** Coal supplied affordable warmth. Industry powered postwar recovery. Officials allowed adaptation time. **Scholarly interpretation.** Weather triggered the episode. Emissions supplied its lethal material. Policy converted disaster into prevention. **Counterevidence and limits.** Inversions are natural events. Continental pollution also contributed. Domestic smoke was not alone. Later death estimates use models. Causation therefore carries ranges. Cleaner heating also mattered. Legislation cannot claim everything. Yet later smogs stayed smaller. Meteorology explains the trap. It does not absolve emissions.",
      "summary": "Inversion converted smoke into crisis.",
