      "id": "aberfan-tip-disaster",
      "title": "Aberfan exposed managed danger",
      "question": "What moved the mountain?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Coal waste accumulated above Aberfan. Seven tips covered the hillside. Tip Seven crossed known springs. Heavy rain saturated its spoil. The mass liquefied suddenly. It descended during October 1966. Pantglas school stood below. The slide killed 144 people. Children numbered 116 victims. Residents had raised concerns. Local knowledge identified water. Board systems failed upward. The official tribunal investigated. It blamed the Coal Board. The report called events preventable. **Benefits claimed.** Hillside tipping reduced transport. Mining supported regional livelihoods. Managers treated movement as manageable. **Scholarly interpretation.** Fragmented knowledge became negligence. Hierarchy separated warnings from power. Technical danger became institutional danger. **Counterevidence and limits.** Rain provided the trigger. Coal waste created exposure. Springs shaped the failure. No single employee designed everything. Responsibility remained organizational. Rescue conditions also mattered. Later memory emphasizes children. Adult victims also deserve notice. Geological explanation supports accountability. It does not replace it. The slide was physical. The disaster was governed.",
