      "id": "love-canal-superfund",
      "title": "Love Canal changed waste law",
      "question": "What did residents prove?",
      "body": "**Documented fact.** Hooker buried chemical waste. Disposal ran through 1953. The company covered the canal. It sold land cheaply. Homes then surrounded the site. A school also opened. Heavy rain raised groundwater. Chemicals reached yards and basements. Residents documented odors and illness. Lois Gibbs organized neighbors. State action began during 1978. Federal emergencies followed twice. Roughly 950 families relocated. Congress created Superfund during 1980. Cleanup then contained the landfill. **Benefits claimed.** Burial matched earlier practice. Land transfer enabled public construction. Officials promised targeted relocation. **Scholarly interpretation.** Resident evidence shifted governance. Visible leakage made risk legible. Love Canal nationalized waste policy. **Counterevidence and limits.** Contamination itself is established. Individual disease attribution differs. Early health studies faced criticism. Sample sizes stayed limited. Comparison groups caused disputes. Some nearby areas became livable. Later studies found mixed results. Precaution still had justification. Exposure pathways were credible. Waiting demanded stronger proof. Residents bore that risk.",
      "summary": "Resident evidence changed waste governance.",
