    {
      "id": "primitive-echo-ostracism",
      "title": "Primitive Echo: exclusion pressure",
      "question": "Why does exclusion sting?",
      "body": "**Documented evidence.** Ostracism threatens basic social needs. Experiments show rapid distress. Brief exclusion can suffice. People report reduced belonging. Control also feels weaker. Self-esteem can fall. Meaning can feel threatened. Everyday studies show varied responses. Mild threats encourage reconnection. Severe threats encourage withdrawal. Antisocial intentions can also rise. Behavior depends upon context. **Evolutionary interpretation.** Ancestral groups supplied protection. Groups also shared food. Exclusion could threaten survival. Sensitivity may therefore persist. This remains an inference. Experiments cannot observe ancestry. **Current echo.** Group chats reproduce exclusion. Workplaces use silent avoidance. Online metrics expose rejection. People may conform afterward. Others may attack instead. **Benefits.** Sensitivity can repair bonds. It can reinforce cooperation. **Harms.** Manipulators can exploit belonging. Groups can silence dissent. Rejected people may disengage. **Counterevidence and limits.** Laboratory games are artificial. Cultural norms alter responses. Personality also changes reactions. Distress proves no adaptation. Evolution explains possibilities. It does not excuse cruelty.",
